---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Duel of Meloch Setup and Teardown: Keeping a 45-Minute Game 45 Minutes"
  description="In a short game, the box is the bottleneck. How to get from shelf to first turn fast."
  game="duel-of-meloch"
  gameTitle="Duel of Meloch"
  category="Guide"
  product="scythe-duel-of-meloch-insert"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Here's a problem short games have that long games don't. If setup and pack-down
    take twelve minutes, that's a rounding error on a three-hour epic — and it's
    more than a quarter of your evening on a 45-minute duel.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Overheads that are invisible in Scythe are painful in Duel of Meloch. The fix is making the box do the sorting for you, so setup is lifting components out rather than separating them." />

  <h2>The Maths of a Short Game</h2>

  <ComparisonTable title="What overhead costs you">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Setup + teardown</th>
          <th>Share of a 120-min Scythe game</th>
          <th>Share of a 45-min duel</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>5 minutes</td>
          <td>4%</td>
          <td>10%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>10 minutes</td>
          <td>8%</td>
          <td><strong>18%</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>15 minutes</td>
          <td>11%</td>
          <td><strong>25%</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <p>
    A quarter of your session spent handling cardboard is the difference between
    "let's play another" and "let's not bother". For games designed to be played
    often, friction at the box is the thing that quietly kills the habit.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="The real cost is the second game">
    Nobody abandons a duel game because one session was slow. They abandon it
    because the pack-down was annoying enough that it doesn't get suggested next
    week. Short games live or die on how easy they are to start.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Where the Time Actually Goes</h2>

  <p>
    Duel of Meloch's component load is modest by Scythe standards, but the two
    asymmetric sides mean you're separating things rather than just distributing
    them:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two different sets of player components</strong> — the Scythe side and the Expeditions side don't share a pile</li>
    <li><strong>Faction pieces</strong>, including any factions you bring in from your Scythe box</li>
    <li><strong>Resource tokens</strong>, which are small, numerous and easy to mix</li>
    <li><strong>Meloch cards and the Triumph Track</strong>, which need to be reachable by both players</li>
    <li><strong>The double-sided map</strong>, which needs deciding on before anything else is placed</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of that is slow if everything is already separated. All of it is slow if
    you're picking tokens out of a communal tray.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting It Down to Two Minutes</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">BIGGEST WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Store components already separated</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">If each side's components live in their own compartment, setup becomes "put this tray in front of you, put that one in front of me". You're distributing, not sorting — and sorting is where the minutes go.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">BIGGEST WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Lids matter more than compartments</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">An open tray is only sorted while the box stays flat. Tip it in a bag and you've undone the work. Covered trays are what make a compact box genuinely portable — which for a 45-minute game is most of the point.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Decide the map side before you unpack</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The board is double-sided. Choosing after you've started laying out components means moving things, and it's the most common self-inflicted delay in setup.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Pack away in setup order</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Everything you put away thoughtfully is time you don't spend next session. It costs nothing at the end of a game and pays every time you open the box.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Keep imported Scythe factions with the duel</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">If you regularly bring a favourite faction over from your Scythe box, storing it in the Meloch box saves opening two games to play one. Just make a note so it doesn't go missing when Scythe next comes out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Play two, then pack down">
    Duels reward back-to-back play — swap sides and run it again. You pay the
    setup cost once for two games, which halves the overhead per session and
    makes for a better evening anyway, since playing both sides is how you
    actually learn the matchup.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Is an Insert Worth It Here?</h2>

  <p>
    For a game you intend to play often, the case is stronger than for a big box.
    Counter-intuitive, but it follows from the maths above: the shorter the game,
    the larger the proportional gain from removing overhead.
  </p>

  <p>
    Add the portability angle and it gets clearer still. A compact duel is the
    game you take to a café, a holiday or a friend's flat — and that's exactly the
    situation where a box full of loose tokens becomes a box full of mixed tokens.
  </p>
</Article>
